A||||5||WisconsinGT||Automatic shift handle
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||07-15-99||05:27 PM||||Do all the stars have to be in alignment for the auto. shift handle to come off? I have taken out the black pin on the back of the handle, but I cannot pull or turn the damn thing off. Would anyone like to share the magic formula? Thanks
Z||000001||batboy||07-15-99||06:13 PM||||When I took my shifter off, I removed the wire clip with a pair of needle-nosed pliers and then the handle pulled right off.
Z||000002||theogre||07-15-99||06:25 PM||||unless some jackass glued it on....... the U shaped clip in a slot on the dash side of the T handle is all that holds it. It pulls straight up after the clip is out.
Z||000003||WisconsinGT||07-15-99||09:22 PM||||Well, I think the Jackass theory is the problem. I cannot for the life of me get this thing to come off. Anyway, I was able to get at the PW switches and replace them. I thank everyone for their help. <p>Let's see, on Monday I am taking this beast in for checkup at the local Pontiac dealer. Is their anything I should be on the lookout for. (I always feel like I am at thier mercy. They can tell me anything, and I will say OK.) I just did the stick the paperclip thing to see what trouble codes where suppressed within. I think I saw a 44 code. So I hope they catch it at the shop. Would it be wise to disconnect the battery and try again? Also, I have Glasspaks on my 88GT. (Previous owner did this modification) It is easy to detect a very distinguishable gas smell when I start the car. I do not know if this is normal with glasspaks or not. Am I in any trouble with them on the car? I do not see a Cad. converter. I live in a state that does not promote a yearly emissions test. Actually, I would love to take them off and put what came from the factory back on. I do not know how expensive that would be.Thanks folks!
Z||000004||theogre||07-16-99||01:50 PM||||44 is a lean mix. (45 is rich) A number of things can cause it. Bad O2 senor, TPS, Injector, low fuel pressure, etc.......
Z||000005||batboy||07-16-99||06:30 PM||||Glass-pack mufflers shouldn't cause any problems other than just being loud. That gas smell might be something else. If you want to replace the muffler, any good muffler shop should be able to cut off the glass-pack and weld on another muffler. That's the easiest and cheapest way. Some shops will tell you that they HAVE to do the whole exhaust or nothing. Tell them goodbye and find a shop that will work with you. Flowmaster is my favorite performance muffler. The sound is halfway between a glass-pack and a regular muffler, a nice deep throaty sound, but not too loud. Personally, I'd leave the cat off if I could get away with it, but you didn't hear that from me. *wink, wink* Technically, there is some federal law about cats, something about having them neutered or spayed. I think? *LOL*
